  such default: a log that stays in the user's devtools console is invisible to you, so something has to carry it off
  the device.

  That matters here because [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m points at [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m, which is a stub. I can't tell from the
  repo whether there's a real backend to receive logs.

  - [1mApp-owned HTTP endpoint, configured not hardcoded[0m — a [38;5;153mPOST /logs[39m your backend owns. Buys full control of format,
    retention, and redaction, and keeps [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m dependency-free. Costs a backend route that may not exist yet, plus
    batching and send-failure handling (a logger that throws during error handling is worse than no logger). The
    endpoint URL is cheap to change; the wire format is not.
  - [1mThird-party service (Sentry, Datadog, LogRocket)[0m — buys grouping, stack-trace symbolication, alerting, and session
    replay for free. Costs your first runtime dependency and a build step this repo doesn't have, sends user data to a
    vendor, and the capture format is theirs, not yours.
  - [1mConsole transport only, remote sink deferred[0m — buys a clean leveled API and consistent structured output now, with a
    documented transport interface to fill in later. Costs: it does not actually solve production debugging, it only
    prepares for it. Honest if no backend exists; a non-answer if one does.

  [1mMy recommendation: the app-owned endpoint, with the console transport as the default and the remote transport enabled
[0m  [1mby config.[0m It keeps the zero-dependency, no-build-step character of this repo, it means nothing breaks if the sink
  isn't ready yet, and it keeps redaction decisions on your side of the wire — which matters, because [38;5;153mapp.js[39m currently
  logs usernames and handles passwords in the same function.
